<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ea-nasir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1750 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ea-nasir was a merchant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> around 1750 BCE who imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a>. He is known from tablets found in his house, above all a letter in which a customer, Nanni, complains angrily about poor-quality copper and rude treatment. The tablet, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, is often called the oldest known written customer complaint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ea-nasir.jpg" alt="Ea-nasir" width="320" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standing_male_worshiper_MET_DT850.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tablets from Ur record Dilmun voyagers, and the Old Babylonian merchant <b>Ea-nasir</b> of Ur, known from a famous letter of complaint about poor-quality copper, traded with Dilmun around 1750 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Isin-Larsa period, when the occupied area reached 140 hectares, the merchant <b>Ea-nasir</b> dealt in copper from Dilmun, shipping ingots that originated in Magan by water to Ur for resale and probably supplying the palace; he was active in the 11th and 19th regnal years of Rim-Sin…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Long-distance trade brought the copper, stone and timber that the plain lacked: at Ur the copper merchant <b>Ea-nasir</b> bought ingots by way of Dilmun, and his house held letters from customers complaining of poor copper.</blockquote><a href="/societies/old-babylonia">Social Hierarchy of Old Babylonia</a></li></ul></section>
